  "response": "<solution>\nimport heapq\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def minMaxWeight(self, n: int, edges: List[List[int]], threshold: int) -> int:\n        # We need to find the minimum possible maximum edge weight such that all nodes can reach node 0.\n        # The threshold condition (<= threshold outgoing edges) is a distractor because as long as \n        # threshold >= 1 (which is guaranteed by constraints), any valid connectivity to node 0 \n        # can be pruned to a directed tree (arborescence) rooted at 0. In such a tree, every node \n        # has exactly 1 outgoing edge (except node 0 which has 0), safely satisfying the threshold.\n        \n        # We model this as finding the minimax path from all nodes to node 0.\n        # This is equivalent to finding the minimax path from node 0 to all other nodes in the reversed graph.\n        \n        adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n        for u, v, w in edges:\n            # Build the reversed graph\n            adj[v].append((u, w))\n            \n        dist = [float('inf')] * n\n        dist[0] = 0\n        pq = [(0, 0)]  # (current_max_weight_on_path, current_node)\n        \n        # Dijkstra's algorithm modified for Bottleneck Path\n        while pq:\n            d, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n            \n            if d > dist[u]:\n                continue\n                \n            for v, w in adj[u]:\n                new_d = max(d, w)\n                if new_d < dist[v]:\n                    dist[v] = new_d\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (new_d, v))\n                    \n        ans = max(dist)\n        return ans if ans != float('inf') else -1\n</solution>\n\nConfidence level: High. The `threshold` constraint is proven to be a non-factor since any valid subgraph can be reduced to a tree directed towards node 0 where the out-degree of all non-root nodes is exactly 1 (which is $\\le$ the minimum possible threshold of 1). The algorithm optimally finds the bottleneck